WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.111
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.111 --> 00:00:12.184
Recently there's been a lot of policy
interest in so-called soda taxes, and

00:00:12.184 --> 00:00:15.528
in the US several cities have
started implementing such taxes.

00:00:15.528 --> 00:00:19.341
And quite a few other cities are thinking
whether to pass such taxes, and

00:00:19.341 --> 00:00:22.042
if they pass them,
how to exactly implement them.

00:00:22.042 --> 00:00:23.755
How to set the tax rate?

00:00:23.755 --> 00:00:26.292
Over which geographic
area to levy the tax?

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:29.369
And which products should
exactly fall under the tax?

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:33.154
And so we were interested in using data
from one of the cities that already

00:00:33.154 --> 00:00:34.458
implemented such a tax,

00:00:34.458 --> 00:00:37.888
in order to understand exactly
how that tax affected consumers.

00:00:37.888 --> 00:00:42.246
Whether it improve nutritional intake,
whether it was able to raise tax revenue.

00:00:42.246 --> 00:00:44.714
And to use those findings to then guide or

00:00:44.714 --> 00:00:48.138
thinking about how to design
soda taxes going forward.

00:00:48.138 --> 00:00:51.956
In our study, we are using data from
Philadelphia which is the first largest

00:00:51.956 --> 00:00:53.997
city that implemented such a soda taxa.

00:00:53.997 --> 00:00:58.016
And it provides us with a very detailed
information across many products and

00:00:58.016 --> 00:01:01.847
a large set of stores starting from
convenience stores to supermarket,

00:01:01.847 --> 00:01:03.004
to wholesale clubs.

00:01:03.004 --> 00:01:07.182
And we observe both the prices and the
quantities that were purchased of sugar

00:01:07.182 --> 00:01:10.925
sweetened beverages, before and
after the texts went into effect.

00:01:10.925 --> 00:01:16.499
[MUSIC]

00:01:16.499 --> 00:01:20.106
Philadelphia in our mind was
particularly useful testbed to look at

00:01:20.106 --> 00:01:21.561
the effects of soda taxes,

00:01:21.561 --> 00:01:25.439
and think about what effect sort of
taxes might have in other localities?

00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:29.928
So far, researchers have mainly use data
from Berkeley where the first sort of of

00:01:29.928 --> 00:01:33.415
tax was introduced in order to
study the effects of such a tax.

00:01:33.415 --> 00:01:36.964
But Berkeley is a relatively small place,
relatively homogeneous, and

00:01:36.964 --> 00:01:40.569
so it's a bit of a question to what
extent those findings would extrapolate

00:01:40.569 --> 00:01:41.560
to other settings.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.392
Philadelphia instead, it's much closer to
the US average in terms of its demographic

00:01:46.392 --> 00:01:48.656
profile, in terms of its obesity rate.

00:01:48.656 --> 00:01:51.662
And it's also quite heterogeneous and
diverse city so

00:01:51.662 --> 00:01:55.975
it allows us to study how consumers in
different neighborhoods with different

00:01:55.975 --> 00:01:59.784
income profiles and different obesity
rates might react to the tax.

00:01:59.784 --> 00:02:05.217
[MUSIC]

00:02:05.217 --> 00:02:09.950
This tax was 1.56 cents per ounce, so
on the typical two liter bottle of Coke,

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:14.204
the price would increase from
about a $1.50 to about $2.50, so

00:02:14.204 --> 00:02:17.035
that's a very substantial
increase in prices.

00:02:17.035 --> 00:02:21.322
Now the tax was levied not on retailers
directly but on distributors.

00:02:21.322 --> 00:02:25.842
So the first thing to study is whether the
price increase was actually passed through

00:02:25.842 --> 00:02:26.680
to consumers.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.493
And we find that the answer
to that question is yes,

00:02:30.493 --> 00:02:34.140
it is essentially a 100% passthrough rate.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:37.256
[MUSIC]

00:02:37.256 --> 00:02:42.005
We find that quantity purchased in stores
in Philadelphia dropped quite dramatically

00:02:42.005 --> 00:02:46.883
by about 46% on average, with sometimes
even larger decreases in supermarkets, and

00:02:46.883 --> 00:02:50.721
some somewhat smaller decreases in
convenience stores to tend to sell

00:02:50.721 --> 00:02:52.236
smaller pack size of good.

00:02:52.236 --> 00:02:57.954
[MUSIC]

00:02:57.954 --> 00:03:00.652
We study in particular
two adjustment margins,

00:03:00.652 --> 00:03:03.561
we looked at do people
switch to untaxed beverages?

00:03:03.561 --> 00:03:07.257
And there's really two classes of
beverages here, people could switch to

00:03:07.257 --> 00:03:11.069
bottled water, or they could switch
to natural juices which aren't taxed,

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:13.961
because they have no artificial
sweetener added to them.

00:03:13.961 --> 00:03:18.811
Interestingly, we find there's almost no
change in demand for bottled water, so

00:03:18.811 --> 00:03:22.777
that doesn't seem to be any
substitution towards those products.

00:03:22.777 --> 00:03:26.195
And there's some modest amount of
substitution to its natural juices,

00:03:26.195 --> 00:03:29.110
which are presumably healthier
along other dimensions, but

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:32.025
they're actually still quite
high in sugar and calories, so

00:03:32.025 --> 00:03:34.452
it doesn't improve things
along that dimension.

00:03:34.452 --> 00:03:37.270
And then finally, and
this is maybe the most interesting and

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.088
striking finding that we
didn't necessarily anticipate,

00:03:40.088 --> 00:03:42.977
is that we see a large amount
of what we call cross-shopping.

00:03:42.977 --> 00:03:47.358
A lot of consumers drive outside of the
city in order to buy the same beverages

00:03:47.358 --> 00:03:49.765
at stores that aren't in the taxed area.

00:03:49.765 --> 00:03:53.435
Now, it's important to bear in mind
that Philadelphia is still a relatively

00:03:53.435 --> 00:03:54.062
small area.

00:03:54.062 --> 00:03:57.393
So you're never more than four
miles away from the city border, so

00:03:57.393 --> 00:04:00.856
it's maybe just quite easy to engage
in that type of cross shopping.

00:04:00.856 --> 00:04:04.758
And so about half of the decrease that
we see in stores in Philadelphia,

00:04:04.758 --> 00:04:07.817
is compensated by a corresponding
increase in stores up

00:04:07.817 --> 00:04:10.043
to six miles outside of the city border.

00:04:10.043 --> 00:04:15.592
[MUSIC]

00:04:15.592 --> 00:04:19.794
Interestingly, what we find is that stores
that are located in lower income areas,

00:04:19.794 --> 00:04:22.825
there's actually less of
a response to the price increase.

00:04:22.825 --> 00:04:26.726
And this was something that initially was
very surprising to us, because you would

00:04:26.726 --> 00:04:29.969
think that Lower income households
tend to be more price sensitive.

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:34.123
Now, one possible explanation for that
is that low income households have less

00:04:34.123 --> 00:04:38.277
access to transport, so it's harder for
them to cross shop at stores outside of

00:04:38.277 --> 00:04:42.381
the city, and hence they can't react
on that particular adjustment margin.

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:46.651
And so what happens is that they simply
continue buying the product at the higher

00:04:46.651 --> 00:04:50.539
price, whereas richer households drive
outside of the city to buy their

00:04:50.539 --> 00:04:53.108
soda at those stores where
the taxes not apply.

00:04:53.108 --> 00:04:58.821
[MUSIC]

00:04:58.821 --> 00:05:02.943
The fact that so many consumers decided
to drive outside of the city to buy sugar

00:05:02.943 --> 00:05:07.266
sweetened beverages, is a discouraging
fact along a few different dimensions.

00:05:07.266 --> 00:05:10.660
So what this large amount of cross
shopping means is that we don't get

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:14.639
an improvement in nutritional intake
because consumers continue to buy the same

00:05:14.639 --> 00:05:15.177
product.

00:05:15.177 --> 00:05:19.628
It leads to lower tax revenue because
purchases outside of the city aren't

00:05:19.628 --> 00:05:23.099
taxed, and to inconveniences
consumers in the process.

00:05:23.099 --> 00:05:27.355
So regardless of whether you're aiming at
improving consumers nutritional intake or

00:05:27.355 --> 00:05:30.912
you're trying to raise tax revenue
that you can use for other purposes in

00:05:30.912 --> 00:05:34.723
the city, the cross shopping makes it
harder to achieve both of those goals.

00:05:34.723 --> 00:05:40.301
[MUSIC].

00:05:40.301 --> 00:05:42.347
Depending on the policy goal you have,

00:05:42.347 --> 00:05:46.315
whether you want to improve nutritional
intake or simply raise tax revenue,

00:05:46.315 --> 00:05:49.477
you might address policy leavers
a little bit differently.

00:05:49.477 --> 00:05:52.777
If you wanted to raise tax revenue,
you want to make it very difficult for

00:05:52.777 --> 00:05:54.106
consumers to avoid the tax.

00:05:54.106 --> 00:05:57.391
So what you want to do is to tax
a broad geographic area, and

00:05:57.391 --> 00:06:02.251
to have a broad product coverage, so it's
difficult for consumers to substitute to

00:06:02.251 --> 00:06:05.615
any other product in either geographic or
product space.

00:06:05.615 --> 00:06:08.568
If instead you are trying to
improve nutritional intake,

00:06:08.568 --> 00:06:12.305
you want to make it hard for consumers
to avoid the tax geographically, but

00:06:12.305 --> 00:06:16.584
you actually do want them specifically to
substitute towards different products in

00:06:16.584 --> 00:06:18.230
particular, healthier ones.

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:22.571
And so if you are trying to improve
nutritional intake, my suggestion would be

00:06:22.571 --> 00:06:26.845
to tax a broader geographic area, but
tax a more narrow range of products that

00:06:26.845 --> 00:06:31.547
actually allows consumers to substitute
towards relatively healthier products.

00:06:31.547 --> 00:06:39.874
[MUSIC].

